Feature: The equilibrium of Towers
  The league can measure Towers itself, and not only one [[captain]]. It plays each [[formation]]
  of a set against each other formation, on both sides of each seed, and gives the
  [[table of a game]]. A good test game has no formation that beats every other, and a wide
  [[equilibrium]].

  Type `cargo run --release -p cognition_league -- nash`. Add `--seeds N` for more games, and
  `--set spire_score=1.5` (or another rule) to change a rule of the game. `--set layout=1` plays
  the chain. The report gives the table, the equilibrium, the formations that have 5 % or more of
  it, the [[cycle share]] of the table, and the formations that beat every other.

  Expectation: The table plays each formation against each other formation
    The valley has eight formations: the computer commander and seven fixed formations.

    When the league makes the table of Towers
    Then the formations in the table are 8
    And the cells of the table are mirrored
    And the report of the table is written

  Expectation: No formation does better against the equilibrium than the equilibrium
    The equilibrium is a mix of formations. It is a Nash equilibrium of the table: the best
    formation against the mix gains nothing on the mix.

    When the league makes the table of Towers
    Then the sum of the equilibrium is 100 %
    And the gain of the best answer to the equilibrium is at most 0.01
